-- title:
--   Mazur's theorem (geometric Hahn–Banach)
-- statement:
--   This is the **geometric form** of the Hahn–Banach theorem. Rather than viewing a linear functional as an element of a dual space, one views it as a hyperplane in the primal space — a change of picture that combines both spaces into one geometric image.
--
--   Let $K$ be a convex set with **nonempty interior** in a real normed linear space $X$, and let $V = x_0 + M$ be a linear variety containing **no interior point** of $K$. Then there is a closed hyperplane containing $V$ and containing no interior point of $K$: there exist a nonzero $x^* \in X^*$ and a constant $c$ with
--
--   $$\langle v, x^*\rangle = c \ \ \text{for } v \in V, \qquad \langle k, x^*\rangle < c \ \ \text{for } k \in \operatorname{int} K, \qquad \langle k, x^*\rangle \le c \ \ \text{for } k \in K.$$
--
--   The proof is where the sublinear form of the extension theorem pays off. Translate so that $0$ is interior to $K$ and let $p$ be the **Minkowski functional** of $K$, $p(x) = \inf\{r > 0 : x/r \in K\}$ — the factor by which $K$ must be inflated to swallow $x$. It is sublinear and continuous, equals the norm when $K$ is the unit ball, and satisfies $p \ge 1$ off the interior. On the subspace generated by $V$ there is a functional $f$ with $V = \{f = 1\}$, and $V$ missing the interior of $K$ gives $f \le p$ there; Hahn–Banach extends it with $F \le p$ on $X$, and $H = \{F = 1\}$ is the required hyperplane.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The variety is parametrized as $x_0 + M$ with $M$ a submodule, so it is nonempty — needed for the functional to be nonzero. The source's display asserts strict inequality on all of $K$; strictness can only hold on the interior, since boundary points of $K$ may lie on the hyperplane, so the conclusion here gives strict inequality on $\operatorname{int} K$ and non-strict on $K$, which is what the source's proof establishes. The Minkowski functional is Mathlib's `gauge`.
-- source:
--   David G. Luenberger, Optimization by Vector Space Methods, John Wiley & Sons, 1969, §5.12, Theorem 1, p. 133

import Mathlib

namespace VectorSpaceOpt

theorem mazur_separation {X : Type} [NormedAddCommGroup X] [NormedSpace ℝ X]
    (K : Set X) (hK : Convex ℝ K) (hKi : (interior K).Nonempty)
    (M : Submodule ℝ X) (x₀ : X)
    (hdisj : ∀ m ∈ M, x₀ + m ∉ interior K) :
    ∃ (f : X →L[ℝ] ℝ) (c : ℝ), f ≠ 0 ∧ (∀ m ∈ M, f (x₀ + m) = c) ∧
      (∀ k ∈ interior K, f k < c) ∧ (∀ k ∈ K, f k ≤ c) := by sorry

end VectorSpaceOpt
